WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.459
<v Lecturer>Okay.</v>

2
00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:02.790
So next we're getting into CloudFormation.

3
00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:04.470
And CloudFormation is not a service

4
00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.820
you have to know in depth

5
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.030
but you need to understand how it works and a few features.

6
00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.340
So this is what I'm going to do in this lecture.

7
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.310
So as we know, CloudFormation is to bring the concept

8
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.433
of infrastructure as code IaC into AWS.

9
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.570
The idea is that by writing your infrastructure as code

10
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.940
you can port it across multiple accounts

11
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.770
and multiple regions so you can reuse

12
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.100
and quickly redeploy your infrastructure anywhere you want.

13
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.010
This is the backbone of the Elastic Beanstalk service

14
00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:34.050
but also the Service Catalog service

15
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.540
and the Serverless Application Model framework.

16
00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.910
So the idea is that it's a low level tool

17
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.270
that other services in AWS may rely on

18
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.520
to perform their actions.

19
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.740
Overall, it's a must know service

20
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.230
when you're a developer, a sysops or a devops.

21
00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.700
But for the SA PRO exam

22
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.410
you still need to understand a few quirks.

23
00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.510
So let's go ahead and learn them.

24
00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.250
Next, how to retain data

25
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.100
when your CloudFormation stack is deleted.

26
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.830
So sometimes you don't want your resources

27
00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:03.780
in CloudFormation to be deleted

28
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.335
because you want to retain the data.

29
00:01:05.335 --> 00:01:08.730
And by default everything gets deleted in CloudFormation.

30
00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:11.552
But you can define a deletion policy on any resource

31
00:01:11.552 --> 00:01:13.170
to control what happens

32
00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.780
when the CloudFormation template is deleted.

33
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.694
So the first one that is extremely obvious

34
00:01:17.694 --> 00:01:21.000
is called deletion policy equals retain.

35
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.700
In this case, you are specifying which resources

36
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.530
you want to preserve or backup

37
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.590
in case CloudFormation deletes the stack.

38
00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.687
To keep a resource, you just specify retain

39
00:01:30.687 --> 00:01:33.635
and it works on any resource or any nested stack.

40
00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:36.351
The second one applies only to less resources

41
00:01:36.351 --> 00:01:39.150
which is called deletion policy equals snapshot.

42
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.600
So in this case we're saying,

43
00:01:41.553 --> 00:01:44.055
okay, you can delete the underlying resource

44
00:01:44.055 --> 00:01:45.690
but please make a snapshot before you do.

45
00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.750
So this applies to all the things that can be snapshotted.

46
00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.690
So EBS volumes, ElastiCache Cluster for Redis

47
00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.300
ElastiCache ReplicationGroup, et cetera, et cetera.

48
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.340
It could be RDS DBInstance

49
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.352
RDS DBCluster or Redshift Cluster.

50
00:01:58.352 --> 00:02:00.390
So anything that can be snapshotted

51
00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.750
usually is supported with deletion policy equals snapshots

52
00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:04.920
for CloudFormation.

53
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.100
And then finally, the default behavior for most resources

54
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.230
is delete policy equals delete.

55
00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.750
And that means that everything is deleted

56
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.734
when you delete your CloudFormation stack.

57
00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:15.953
But there is an exception

58
00:02:15.953 --> 00:02:18.954
if you're using an RDS DBCluster resources

59
00:02:18.954 --> 00:02:21.420
the default policy is to use snapshots,

60
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.068
and if you want to delete an S3 bucket

61
00:02:23.068 --> 00:02:26.010
through CloudFormation you need to make sure

62
00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.350
the bucket is going to be first entirely empty

63
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.620
of all its content, otherwise you're going to get a failure.

64
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.970
But from an exam perspective

65
00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.778
remember you can set a deletion policy

66
00:02:34.778 --> 00:02:39.450
to control how CloudFormation deletes or not resources.

67
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.709
CloudFormation can do custom resources using Lambda.

68
00:02:43.709 --> 00:02:46.440
So the customer resources help you address

69
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.217
any of the following use cases.

70
00:02:48.217 --> 00:02:51.415
For example, AWS just released this new service

71
00:02:51.415 --> 00:02:54.390
but it is not yet supported by CloudFormation.

72
00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.250
Or you have an on-premise resource

73
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.149
that you would like to manage through CloudFormation

74
00:02:58.149 --> 00:03:00.480
or you would like would like to empty an S3 bucket

75
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.350
before deleting it, or you would like to get an AMI ID

76
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.960
before applying it in your CloudFormation templates

77
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.550
or really anything you want.

78
00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.618
So the idea is that these custom resources in CloudFormation

79
00:03:10.618 --> 00:03:13.920
is going to be backed by a Lambda function

80
00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.780
that you can create and that Lambda function

81
00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.750
is going to be responsible for doing whatever you want.

82
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.070
Will it be creating a new resource

83
00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.540
or managing your on-premise resource

84
00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.340
or emptying your S3 bucket?

85
00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:24.810
All these kind of things.

86
00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:26.250
And so anytime you create update

87
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.530
or delete your confirmation stack

88
00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.570
then the Lambda function will be invoked

89
00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.340
and that Lambda function will do

90
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.580
based on what CloudFormation tell it, it just happened.

91
00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.410
The CloudFormation, the Lambda function you have to program

92
00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.533
will do API calls to whatever you want

93
00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:43.260
to manage properly your custom resources.

94
00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.170
So the idea is as soon as you need to go a little bit crazy

95
00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:48.733
out of the box usage of confirmation

96
00:03:48.733 --> 00:03:50.870
you have to use custom resources.

97
00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.617
Next we have StackSets.

98
00:03:52.617 --> 00:03:55.740
So using StackSets, you can create, update

99
00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.140
or delete stacks across multiple accounts

100
00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.540
and regions within a single operation.

101
00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.613
So you have two types of accounts for StackSets.

102
00:04:02.613 --> 00:04:04.230
You have the administrator accounts

103
00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.629
that create these StackSets

104
00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:08.730
and then the trusted accounts can create, update

105
00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.431
and delete stack instances from these StackSets.

106
00:04:12.431 --> 00:04:14.820
So when you update a stack set

107
00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.830
and that's to the whole power of StackSets,

108
00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:20.160
then every associated stack instances

109
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.160
are is going to be updated through all the accounts

110
00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.420
in all the region.

111
00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.760
So they gives you one way to manage stacks

112
00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.880
across multiple accounts and multiple regions.

113
00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.400
There is also one last feature you need to know about

114
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.950
which is called automatic deployments.

115
00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.474
So in case you're using AWS organization,

116
00:04:37.474 --> 00:04:40.636
then you can automatically deploy a StackSets

117
00:04:40.636 --> 00:04:43.313
to new accounts and it is extremely powerful

118
00:04:43.313 --> 00:04:44.607
to provision whatever you want as a minimal configuration

119
00:04:46.598 --> 00:04:48.136
of a new account.

120
00:04:48.136 --> 00:04:50.460
So CloudFormation StackSets, again as an admin account

121
00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:53.760
and then the same stack can be deployed either in account A

122
00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.720
in two different regions or account B, a separate account.

123
00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:00.513
And remember the automatic deployment feature.

124
00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:03.030
Next we have Drift.

125
00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.170
So what is Drift?

126
00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.510
Well, we know that we can create infrastructure

127
00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:07.594
using CloudFormation

128
00:05:07.594 --> 00:05:12.240
but it doesn't protect against manual configuration changes

129
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.190
of your CloudFormation resources.

130
00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:17.250
And therefore your resources, if they change configuration,

131
00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.170
we call them drift.

132
00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:20.994
So how to know whether they're,

133
00:05:20.994 --> 00:05:21.827
whether or not they have drifted?

134
00:05:21.827 --> 00:05:23.250
Well, you can use CloudFormation Drift.

135
00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.260
And CloudFormation Drift is going to evaluate

136
00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.700
all your resources and have a look at the configuration

137
00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.350
provided by CloudFormation

138
00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:33.180
and maybe the actual configuration

139
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.587
after someone modified it manually.

140
00:05:35.587 --> 00:05:38.310
And then there's gonna be a comparison algorithm.

141
00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.027
And then automatically it will tell you,

142
00:05:40.027 --> 00:05:42.330
"Well, these resources have drifted."

143
00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.712
So this can be detected on an entire stack

144
00:05:44.712 --> 00:05:48.282
or individual resources within a stack.

145
00:05:48.282 --> 00:05:50.820
Okay, so there's just one template issue about

146
00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.850
it's the integration of Secrets Manager in CloudFormation.

147
00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:55.020
So here's what the template is.

148
00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.820
So in the first part of the template

149
00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.666
we generate a secret from Secrets Manager.

150
00:05:59.666 --> 00:06:01.830
In the second part of the template,

151
00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:06.492
you use a reference, a subfunction to find the secret

152
00:06:06.492 --> 00:06:09.930
and pass it in into your RDS database instance.

153
00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.167
Okay? So this is how you inject the secrets.

154
00:06:12.167 --> 00:06:15.630
The password is created into the instance of RDS.

155
00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.070
And then finally, the last part

156
00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:19.710
is to create a secret target attachment

157
00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:23.010
to link the secret to the RDS database instance.

158
00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:25.380
And therefore when the secret rotates

159
00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.810
so will the password in the RDS database.

160
00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.900
Then we have a resource imports for CloudFormation.

161
00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.570
So this allows you to import existing resources

162
00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.991
that you've created in AWS, outside of CloudFormation

163
00:06:36.991 --> 00:06:40.590
into existing or new CloudFormation stacks.

164
00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:42.300
So you don't need to actually delete

165
00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:43.560
and recreate the resources

166
00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.300
as part of a new CloudFormation stack.

167
00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.040
You can just import them.

168
00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.050
So during the import operation,

169
00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.929
you will need to create a template

170
00:06:50.929 --> 00:06:54.093
that describes exactly the entire stack.

171
00:06:54.093 --> 00:06:56.270
That means the original stack resources

172
00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.476
and the target resources to import.

173
00:06:58.476 --> 00:07:00.090
You will also make sure

174
00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.210
that every target resource has a unique identifier.

175
00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.980
For example, for S3 buckets,

176
00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:08.220
you need to clearly outline the name of the bucket,

177
00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.250
so the bucket name.

178
00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:11.432
So let's say we have a S3 bucket, MyBucket

179
00:07:11.432 --> 00:07:14.850
and we want to implement it and import it into a stack.

180
00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.634
So we're going to create this template

181
00:07:16.634 --> 00:07:18.369
with the resources to import

182
00:07:18.369 --> 00:07:22.530
and outline exactly the S3 bucket, including its name.

183
00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.312
Then we'll import resources into CloudFormation

184
00:07:25.312 --> 00:07:27.570
which is going to create a stack.

185
00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:28.992
And this stack will just include

186
00:07:28.992 --> 00:07:32.852
that already existing S3 bucket into the stack resources

187
00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:35.610
and will be managed then through CloudFormation.

188
00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:37.320
But it's actually not gonna go

189
00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:39.060
and delete and recreate that resource for you.

190
00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:40.620
It's just going to import it.

191
00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:42.094
So each resource you import

192
00:07:42.094 --> 00:07:45.571
you must define as well a deletion policy with any value

193
00:07:45.571 --> 00:07:48.828
and an identifier, and you cannot of course import

194
00:07:48.828 --> 00:07:52.352
the same resources into multiple stacks.

195
00:07:52.352 --> 00:07:53.652
So that's it for this lecture.

196
00:07:53.652 --> 00:07:57.183
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

